% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------
Insider threats and stealthy account compromises represent persistent blind spots for perimeter-centric security architectures, largely because attackers operating on valid credentials can sustain individually innocuous actions that collectively constitute a meaningful departure from an established behavioral identity.
Existing User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) platforms detect discrete anomalies but lack the mechanisms needed to track long-range identity continuity, distinguish natural behavioral evolution from adversarial drift, or adapt access decisions in real time.
This paper presents \textit{CogniTrace}, a behavioral identity drift detection framework that addresses these gaps through five synergistic contributions: (i) a Temporal Sequence Autoencoder that maps multi-modal session features onto compact behavioral identity embeddings; (ii) Behavioral Transition Graph Modeling, which encodes workflow continuity as a per-user Markov-like state graph and measures graph-level divergence from trusted baselines; (iii) a variance-adaptive Exponential Moving Average trust engine augmented with trust recovery and persistent risk memory; (iv) an Adaptive Baseline Refreshing mechanism that anchors identity profiles exclusively to verified low-risk windows, preventing gradual drift from corrupting baselines; and (v) an Explainable Drift Attribution module powered by SHAP values that translates numerical risk scores into analyst-interpretable evidence.
Evaluation on the Behavioral Enterprise Simulation Environment (BESE), a purpose-built multi-persona simulation platform, yields an ROC-AUC of 0.95, an F1-score of 0.92, and a false positive rate below 5\%, while ablation experiments confirm that each architectural component contributes independently to detection capability.
CogniTrace offers a scalable, production-oriented pathway toward continuous behavioral authentication in distributed enterprise environments.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Behavioral Identity Drift, Insider Threat Detection, Adaptive Trust Evolution,
UEBA, Continuous Behavioral Authentication, Behavioral Transition Graphs,
Concept Drift, Explainable AI, Human-Centric Security, Cybersecurity
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------

The expansion of hybrid work models, cloud-native infrastructure, and AI-mediated collaboration tools has fundamentally altered enterprise attack surfaces.
Perimeter-centric security mechanisms offer diminishing effectiveness when adversaries already possess authenticated access credentials.
Recent insider threat studies highlight that credential misuse and privilege abuse remain dominant contributors to enterprise security breaches \cite{b1}, exposing a critical limitation in existing security systems: most deployed defenses detect \textit{what} an account does at a specific point in time but fail to continuously verify \textit{who} the account behaviorally represents across time.

Conventional UEBA systems rely on threshold-based rules, short-window statistical baselines, and supervised anomaly classifiers \cite{b2}.
These approaches evaluate sessions largely in isolation, making them ineffective against gradual behavioral compromise.
An adversary who incrementally shifts login timing, expands resource-access scope, or subtly alters workflow behavior across many sessions can remain below per-session anomaly thresholds while cumulatively executing a significant intrusion \cite{b3}.
As a result, behavioral identity may drift substantially without triggering explicit alerts.

This work argues that detecting such slow-burn threats requires a transition from \textit{event-level anomaly detection} toward \textit{continuous behavioral identity verification}.
The proposed framework, \textbf{CogniTrace}, addresses this challenge by maintaining adaptive behavioral identity profiles, measuring divergence across both embedding space and workflow transition structure, and continuously evolving trust according to long-term behavioral consistency rather than isolated anomalous events.

The primary contributions of this work are as follows.
\begin{itemize}
  \item A formal threat model defining insider abuse and credential-compromise scenarios with explicit trust boundaries.
  
  \item A Temporal Sequence Autoencoder that generates sequence-aware behavioral identity embeddings from multi-session telemetry streams.
  
  \item Behavioral Transition Graph Modeling that quantifies workflow divergence using per-user transition probability graphs.
  
  \item A Drift Stability Index and Adaptive Baseline Refreshing mechanism for concept drift-resilient behavioral identity tracking.
  
  \item A trust-aware behavioral intelligence framework integrating adaptive trust evolution, persistent risk memory, and trust recovery dynamics.
  
  \item An explainable risk attribution engine grounded in SHAP values for analyst-interpretable behavioral drift reasoning.
\end{itemize}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Related Work}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------

Research on insider threat detection has evolved through rule-based, statistical, and machine learning-driven behavioral analytics frameworks.
Early rule-based systems \cite{b4} relied on manually engineered heuristics and static access-control policies, providing interpretability but limited adaptability against evolving behavioral patterns.
Subsequent statistical approaches introduced clustering, probabilistic modeling, and unsupervised anomaly detection over enterprise telemetry streams \cite{b5}, enabling identification of deviating sessions without explicit attack signatures.
However, these methods typically modeled sessions independently, making them ineffective against gradual behavioral drift and low-and-slow compromise strategies.

Machine learning-based approaches later incorporated temporal and relational behavioral modeling.
Deep learning frameworks applied to structured cybersecurity telemetry demonstrated that sequence-aware analysis significantly improves anomaly detection recall compared to static feature aggregation \cite{b6}.
Graph-oriented frameworks such as Log2Vec \cite{b7} further captured heterogeneous workflow relationships and lateral movement patterns within enterprise environments.
Trust-aware authentication systems \cite{b8} introduced contextual trust scoring for adaptive access control, while transformer-based enterprise log analysis models achieved strong benchmark performance through long-range sequence learning \cite{b9}.
Despite these advances, existing systems remain predominantly event-centric, classifying sessions or actions as anomalous without maintaining a persistent behavioral identity continuity signal across time.

A consistent research limitation therefore emerges across prior work:
\textit{existing frameworks do not jointly model longitudinal behavioral identity embeddings, workflow transition drift, adaptive trust evolution, concept drift-resilient baselines, and explainable behavioral risk attribution within a unified continuous authentication architecture.}
Consequently, gradual identity compromise, behavioral impersonation, and slow-burn insider attacks can remain operationally indistinguishable from legitimate user evolution.

CogniTrace is designed to address this gap through an integrated behavioral identity intelligence framework that combines temporal behavioral embeddings, Behavioral Transition Graph Modeling, variance-adaptive trust evolution, concept drift-aware baseline adaptation, and SHAP-grounded explainable risk reasoning for continuous behavioral identity verification.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Threat Model}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Scope and Assumptions}

CogniTrace operates within enterprise environments where all users authenticate through centralized identity providers and endpoint telemetry—including login events, file operations, network activity, and command execution—is collected continuously.
The framework rests on four foundational assumptions: (1) adversaries hold valid credentials at the time of interaction, either through prior compromise or insider status; (2) telemetry collection infrastructure is outside the adversary's tamper scope; (3) behavioral log records cannot be fully erased or altered without detectable metadata inconsistencies; and (4) continuous session-level monitoring is operationally feasible.

The framework's trust boundary is deliberately asymmetric: \textit{system-level telemetry is treated as trusted}, while \textit{the behavioral consistency of authenticated principals is treated as untrusted and subject to continuous validation}.
This boundary acknowledges that authentication tokens prove identity at a moment in time but provide no ongoing assurance about behavioral legitimacy.

\subsection{Threat Categories}

Table~\ref{tab:threats} enumerates the six threat categories within the model's scope.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Threat Categories Addressed by CogniTrace}
\label{tab:threats}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{2.1cm}|p{5.4cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Threat Type} & \textbf{Operational Description} \\
\hline
Compromised Credentials & External attacker operates under a stolen but valid identity, deviating subtly from the legitimate user's workflow patterns. \\
\hline
Insider Abuse & Authorized employee intentionally misuses access privileges, typically through gradual scope expansion. \\
\hline
Behavioral Impersonation & Adversary reverse-engineers a target's workflow to blend in, producing partial mimicry that diverges at the graph transition level. \\
\hline
Slow Privilege Escalation & Access scope widens incrementally across many sessions, staying below per-session anomaly thresholds. \\
\hline
Low-and-Slow Exfiltration & Small-volume data transfers distributed over extended periods evade volume-based detectors. \\
\hline
Social Engineering Drift & A manipulated user's behavior shifts under external influence, producing a mixed-intent drift pattern. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\subsection{Adversary Model}

The adversary is assumed to be patient, knowledgeable about general UEBA detection thresholds, and capable of inspecting their own prior activity logs if insider access permits.
The adversary is \textit{not} assumed to have knowledge of the specific embedding architecture, transition graph structure, or trust evolution parameters used by CogniTrace.
Under this model, evasion requires maintaining near-perfect behavioral fidelity at both the feature-vector level and the workflow sequencing level simultaneously, a constraint that becomes increasingly difficult to sustain over multi-session attack campaigns.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\section{CogniTrace: Proposed Framework}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{System Overview}

CogniTrace is structured as a nine-stage continuous processing pipeline.
Raw telemetry generated by monitored endpoints enters a streaming ingestion layer, passes through feature extraction and temporal embedding, undergoes identity consistency evaluation across both embedding and graph dimensions, feeds a trust evolution engine, and ultimately drives an adaptive policy layer.
Every stage is stateless with respect to raw events but stateful with respect to per-user behavioral models, enabling horizontal scaling across user populations.

\subsection{System Architecture}

The end-to-end data flow is organized as follows.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Telemetry Stream:} Endpoint logs, network flow records, and authentication events enter the pipeline via Apache Kafka topics partitioned by user identifier, providing ordered, fault-tolerant stream ingestion with sub-second delivery.
  \item \textbf{Behavior Collection Engine:} A Kafka consumer group normalizes heterogeneous log formats into a unified session schema, deduplicates events within configurable tumbling windows, and forwards structured session records downstream.
  \item \textbf{Feature Engineering Layer:} Multi-category behavioral features are extracted per session window and assembled into a fixed-dimensional feature vector.
  \item \textbf{Temporal Sequence Autoencoder:} The feature sequence for a configurable sliding window of recent sessions is encoded into a compact behavioral identity embedding.
  \item \textbf{Behavioral Transition Graph Engine:} A per-user Markov-like state graph is maintained and updated; graph drift relative to the trusted baseline is computed at each session boundary.
  \item \textbf{Identity Consistency \& Drift Engine:} Cosine-based embedding drift, graph drift, drift acceleration, and the Drift Stability Index are computed and combined.
  \item \textbf{Adaptive Trust Evolution Module:} The user's trust score is updated via variance-adaptive EMA, with trust recovery applied when risk subsides.
  \item \textbf{Risk Classification \& Explainability Engine:} XGBoost produces a calibrated risk probability; SHAP values attribute risk contribution to individual features.
  \item \textbf{Adaptive Policy Layer:} Risk probability and trust trajectory jointly determine access policy action.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Feature Engineering}

Six behavioral signal categories are extracted from each session window, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:features}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Behavioral Feature Space}
\label{tab:features}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Category} & \textbf{Representative Features} \\
\hline
Temporal & Login hour, session duration, inter-session gap \\
\hline
Access & File access count, resource scope, data volume \\
\hline
Command & Command entropy, execution frequency \\
\hline
Navigation & Resource traversal depth, path novelty score \\
\hline
Privilege & Escalation attempts, elevated-access duration \\
\hline
Interaction Sequence & Action n-gram distribution, transition entropy \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

All features are standardized using an online z-score normalizer that updates incrementally, preserving scale invariance as user behavioral baselines shift over calendar time.

\subsection{Behavioral Identity Embeddings via Temporal Autoencoder}

Rather than encoding each session independently, a \textit{Temporal Sequence Autoencoder} (TSA) processes a rolling window of $W$ consecutive session feature vectors.
The encoder stacks two LSTM layers over the input sequence before projecting through a dense bottleneck; the decoder reconstructs the sequence from the latent code.
The reconstruction objective forces the latent representation to capture sequential dependencies—interaction ordering, temporal rhythms, and workflow continuity—that a frame-wise autoencoder would discard.

The behavioral identity embedding for user $u$ at time $t$ is:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{E}_u(t) = \mathrm{Enc}_\theta\!\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-W+1},\, \mathbf{x}_{t-W+2},\, \ldots,\, \mathbf{x}_t\right)
  \label{eq:embedding}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{x}_\tau \in \mathbb{R}^d$ is the feature vector for session $\tau$ and $\mathrm{Enc}_\theta$ denotes the encoder parameterized by $\theta$.
The latent dimension is set to 64 in all experiments.

\subsection{Behavioral Transition Graph Modeling}

A unique workflow fingerprint develops over many sessions as each user repeatedly traverses a characteristic set of system states: login, file access, database query, report generation, and logout, for example.
Attackers who operate on compromised accounts or exhibit insider intent gradually diverge from these habitual pathways.
CogniTrace captures this workflow structure through a per-user \textit{Behavioral Transition Graph} $\mathcal{G}_u = (V_u, E_u)$, where $V_u$ is the set of distinct behavioral states observed for user $u$ and $E_u$ encodes the empirical transition probabilities between states.

The transition probability from state $v_i$ to state $v_j$ is estimated as:
\begin{equation}
  P(v_i \to v_j) = \frac{\mathrm{count}(v_i \to v_j)}{\sum_{k}\,\mathrm{count}(v_i \to v_k)}
  \label{eq:transition}
\end{equation}

At each evaluation epoch, a current transition distribution $P^{\mathrm{current}}$ is estimated from the most recent session window and compared against the trusted baseline $P^{\mathrm{baseline}}$.
The resulting \textit{graph drift score} is:
\begin{equation}
  D_{\mathrm{graph}}(t) = \sum_{i} \left| P_i^{\mathrm{baseline}} - P_i^{\mathrm{current}} \right|
  \label{eq:graphdrift}
\end{equation}

Graph drift complements embedding drift by capturing workflow sequencing inconsistencies that aggregate feature statistics alone cannot detect.

\subsection{Identity Drift Detection and Drift Stability Index}

\subsubsection{Embedding Drift}
The cosine distance between the current identity embedding and the trusted baseline embedding yields the primary drift score:
\begin{equation}
  D_{\mathrm{id}}(t) = 1 - \frac{\mathbf{E}_u(t) \cdot \mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{baseline}}}
                                  {\|\mathbf{E}_u(t)\|\;\|\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{baseline}}\|}
  \label{eq:iddrift}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Drift Acceleration}
The rate of change of identity drift across consecutive evaluation epochs captures momentum in behavioral departure:
\begin{equation}
  A_{\mathrm{drift}}(t) = \frac{D_{\mathrm{id}}(t) - D_{\mathrm{id}}(t-1)}{\Delta t}
  \label{eq:accel}
\end{equation}
Sustained positive acceleration across three or more epochs constitutes a high-confidence indicator of progressive compromise even when the absolute drift value remains moderate.

\subsubsection{Drift Stability Index}
Behavioral consistency across time is quantified by the \textit{Drift Stability Index}:
\begin{equation}
  S_{\mathrm{drift}} = 1 - \sigma\!\left(D_{\mathrm{id},\,t-K:t}\right)
  \label{eq:stability}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes the standard deviation of the last $K$ drift scores.

\subsubsection{Adaptive Baseline Refreshing}
Human behavior evolves legitimately over time due to role changes, project transitions, and organizational restructuring.
To prevent this natural evolution from being misclassified as malicious drift, CogniTrace refreshes the trusted baseline exclusively from sessions whose risk score falls below a low-risk threshold $\tau_r$:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{baseline}}(t+1) =
    \begin{cases}
      \beta\,\mathbf{E}_u(t) + (1-\beta)\,\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{baseline}}(t) & \text{if } R(t) < \tau_r \\
      \mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{baseline}}(t) & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
  \label{eq:baseline}
\end{equation}
The retention factor $\beta$ is set conservatively ($\beta = 0.05$) to allow gradual assimilation of legitimate behavioral evolution while resisting adversarial drift from corrupting the reference profile.

\subsection{Adaptive Trust Evolution and Risk Memory}

\subsubsection{EMA Trust Update}
Trust evolves continuously according to a variance-adaptive EMA:
\begin{equation}
  T_u(t) = \alpha(t)\bigl(1 - D_{\mathrm{id}}(t)\bigr) + \bigl(1 - \alpha(t)\bigr)\,T_u(t-1)
  \label{eq:trust}
\end{equation}
The smoothing coefficient $\alpha(t)$ increases with the variance of recent drift scores, making the trust response more reactive during periods of behavioral volatility and more stable during consistent behavioral phases.

\subsubsection{Trust Recovery Dynamics}
When a previously flagged user resumes demonstrably benign behavior, the trust score is rehabilitated proportionally to the prevailing safety of current activity:
\begin{equation}
  T_u^{\mathrm{rec}}(t) = T_u(t) + \lambda\bigl(1 - R(t)\bigr)
  \label{eq:recovery}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ is a recovery rate hyperparameter and $R(t)$ is the current risk probability.
Recovery accrues only when $R(t) < \tau_r$, ensuring that trust rehabilitation cannot be exploited as a reset mechanism by an attacker who briefly simulates normal behavior.

\subsubsection{Risk Memory}
Point-in-time risk estimates are smoothed into a persistent memory signal that weights recent risk proportionally while retaining evidence of prior elevated-risk episodes:
\begin{equation}
  M_{\mathrm{risk}}(t) = \eta\,R(t) + (1 - \eta)\,M_{\mathrm{risk}}(t-1)
  \label{eq:riskmem}
\end{equation}
This prevents an attacker from temporarily suppressing risk scores to evade detection while a sustained underlying threat persists in the memory trace.

\subsection{Risk Classification and Explainable Drift Attribution}

The final risk probability for each session epoch is computed by an XGBoost classifier trained on the concatenation of embedding drift, graph drift, drift acceleration, stability index, anomaly score (from an Isolation Forest applied to raw feature vectors), trust score, risk memory:
\begin{equation}
  R(t) = f_{\mathrm{XGB}}\!\left(D_{\mathrm{id}},\,D_{\mathrm{graph}},\,A_{\mathrm{drift}},\,S_{\mathrm{drift}},\,A_{\mathrm{iso}},\,T_u,\,M_{\mathrm{risk}})
  \label{eq:risk}
\end{equation}

For every prediction, SHAP values are computed to quantify the marginal contribution of each input feature to the risk score.
The Explainable Drift Attribution module surfaces the top three contributing factors in natural language, for example: \textit{``Top contributing behavioral factors are surfaced through SHAP attribution.''}
These attributions are logged alongside raw scores in the analyst dashboard, enabling human reviewers to rapidly triage high-priority alerts.

\subsection{Adaptive Policy Intelligence Engine}

Policy decisions are conditioned on the joint state of the risk probability, the trust trajectory (rising, stable, or falling), and the current drift acceleration rather than on threshold crossings of a single scalar.
Table~\ref{tab:policy} maps operational conditions to policy actions.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Adaptive Policy Condition-Action Mapping}
\label{tab:policy}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{3.8cm}|p{3.7cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Operational Condition} & \textbf{Policy Action} \\
\hline
Low risk, stable trust & Allow; standard logging \\
\hline
Mild drift, moderate risk & Warn; generate analyst alert \\
\hline
Sustained drift, declining trust & Enforce adaptive MFA at next sensitive operation \\
\hline
Accelerated drift, high risk memory & Restrict; limit access to read-only resources \\
\hline
Critical instability, near-zero trust & Terminate session; escalate to SOC \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experimental Setup and Results}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection{Behavioral Enterprise Simulation Environment}

Because real enterprise insider threat datasets are largely unavailable due to privacy constraints \cite{b11}, evaluation is conducted on the \textit{Behavioral Enterprise Simulation Environment} (BESE), a purpose-built multi-agent simulation platform.
BESE models five archetypal user personas whose behavioral parameters are independently configurable, as detailed in Table~\ref{tab:personas}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{BESE User Personas}
\label{tab:personas}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{5.1cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Persona} & \textbf{Behavioral Characteristics} \\
\hline
Stable Employee & Fixed login window, repetitive access sequences, low behavioral variance across sessions \\
\hline
Power User & High action volume, diverse resource access, naturally elevated drift scores that peer normalization accommodates \\
\hline
Compromised User & Progressive embedding drift injected over a configurable attack ramp length (20–40 sessions) \\
\hline
Insider Attacker & Deliberate privilege escalation and data access expansion with partial workflow mimicry \\
\hline
Socially Engineered User & Mixed-intent drift alternating between normal and atypical sessions, simulating external manipulation \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

The simulation generates 15,000 sessions across 300 synthetic user instances.
Drift is injected in the Compromised User and Insider Attacker personas through parameterized perturbation of feature distributions and transition probability matrices.
Attack prevalence is set at 15\% of users to reflect conservative insider threat base rates reported in industry surveys \cite{b12}.

\subsection{Experimental Configuration}

All experiments are conducted on an Intel Core i7 workstation with 16\,GB RAM running Python 3.10.
The TSA encoder is implemented in PyTorch with the Adam optimizer ($\text{lr} = 10^{-3}$, 50 epochs).
XGBoost is trained with 300 estimators, maximum depth 6, and learning rate 0.05.
The Isolation Forest contamination parameter is set to 0.1.
Session window size is $W = 10$; risk memory decay is $\eta = 0.4$; recovery rate is $\lambda = 0.02$; and baseline retention is $\beta = 0.05$.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

Performance is assessed using ROC-AUC, Precision, Recall, F1-score, and False Positive Rate (FPR).
In addition, \textit{Drift Detection Latency} measures the mean number of sessions elapsed between the start of a simulated attack ramp and the first high-confidence alert, and the \textit{Trust Stability Index} quantifies score consistency for normal users over the evaluation window.

