\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{2.8cm}|p{4.7cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Limitation} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
BESE-based evaluation & Real enterprise behavioral data is unavailable at scale; BESE approximates but cannot fully replicate organizational complexity \\
\hline
Behavioral ambiguity & Legitimate role transitions can produce drift signatures similar to slow-burn compromise; the baseline refresher mitigates but does not eliminate this overlap \\
\hline
Computational overhead & Maintaining per-user TSA models and transition graphs at large population scales requires careful resource planning \\
\hline
Longitudinal validation absent & Multi-month deployment studies are needed to validate trust recovery dynamics and concept drift management under real operational conditions \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
% ---------------------------------------------------------------

This paper presented CogniTrace, a behavioral identity drift detection framework that advances insider threat detection from event-level anomaly scoring toward continuous identity continuity verification.
By combining a Temporal Sequence Autoencoder with Behavioral Transition Graph Modeling, variance-adaptive trust evolution, concept drift-resilient baselines, risk memory, and SHAP-grounded explainability, the framework achieves an ROC-AUC of 0.95 and an F1-score of 0.92 on the BESE benchmark with sub-100\,ms end-to-end processing latency at enterprise user scales.
Ablation experiments confirm that each architectural component contributes independently to detection capability, while comparative analysis demonstrates that CogniTrace provides a broader behavioral intelligence capability surface than existing UEBA approaches.

Future work will explore Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) for higher-order workflow reasoning, federated trust intelligence for cross-organization behavioral threat sharing, and online continual learning for adaptive behavioral identity modeling in evolving enterprise environments.

